From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Mon, 2 May 94 4:09:39 WET DST
Subject: [#94-5-13] Filtering Techniques (filtering the flood)

Anton Sherwood writes:
[description of rating vector method deleted]

  This has been reinvented over and over. Carl Feynmann, myself, and
I think even David Friedman, have proposed various forms of it.
One problem with the one you propose is that it only filters for
author. While I might enjoy Tim Starr's (sorry to use you Tim
as a guinea pig) posts on philosophy or politics, I might not like his posts 
about quantum mechanics. Thus, I want to filter the give high priority
to posts from Tim on objectivist philosophy, but for debates about
the Everett Hypothesis, I favor Mike Price.

  One of my ideas is the "dynamic moderator" approach. Here's how
it works. By rating various messages, an "interest vector" is created
which captures your interests in various threads. The same thing happens
for everyone else. In the 'dynamic moderation' phase, the list software
looks for people who are close to you in interest-space. These people,
whose absolute distance is within some radius, and their rating,
is used to compute a more honest appraisal of the value of a post.

  It is based on the idea that people whose interests are closer to yours
rate messages with the same bias you do and therefore their ratings 
should be more heavily weighted. To use Tim as an example again,
Tim's interest might be more in favor of threads based on economics
and philosophy, on the other hand, Mike might be more interested in
space, physics, and genetic algorithms. When Tim rates a thread
on genetic algorithms, it will obviously be lower than a person whose's
primary field of interest is GA. Thus, it would be unfair to give
Tim's rating equal weighting with Mike's if YOU are filtering in favor
of GA (because that's your interest) On the other hand, if you are
filtering for philosophy, you would want to add more weight to Tim's
ratings and not Mike's.

  The central flaw of democracy is that all votes are given equal footing.
Someone who spent years debating and lobbying around a policy issue
is given the same weight as a homeless man flipping a coin and pulling
a lever. Dynamic moderation eliminates the irrelevent votes by finding
those people in your interest group first.

  The moderation is dynamic because it is chosen at run(post) time and it is
constantly changing. When you receive a message approved because it passed
the dynamic moderation phase, and you hated it, you provide a relevance
rating. This then adjusts your rating vector slightly so that over
time, the software chooses different sample groups.

  My serious concern is conservatism in the interest-vector. There
has to be some, but how much? What I mean is, when the relevance adjusts
the interest-vector, by what factor should it adjust it? Setting it
too low creates a situation where the interest-vector changes too
slowly (conservative) Setting it too high leads to a chaotically
fluctuating one where your "interests" change radically almost
after every post. You don't want a single bad rating to cause the
list software to think you hate nanotechnology threads and thus
filter them out totally.

  My gut feeling is that they should change fast at first but then slow down.
My reasoning being that as you spend more time on the list, you want
tighter filtering for your interests, and you become more (gulp!) dogmatic
in your viewpoint.

  Another problem is the chicken-and-egg paradox. Obviously, for
dynamic moderation to work, some people have to see the post, and rate
it, before you do. Thus there would be one set of people, who receive
posts in real time and rate them, and other people who receive them
filtered, perhaps by digest, and provide relevance. 

  One solution is to provide a second filtering mechanism to those
who get mail in real time. One of my ideas is to use the author's
interest-vector as an initial "rating" Then, you get the message
if your interests are close to theirs. This is equivalent to Anton's
proposal. Another idea is to use another filtering system I have
invented tentatively called Parametric Differentiation. Basically,
I keep another set of vectors in "message parametric space" Parametrics
are based on various data such as author, subject, prefix, thread,
size, date, keywords, and even the body of the message. Each message
is converted to a parametric vector and distances are computed
vs. other messages. Your ratings determine a basis(*) in this
space, and each parameterized message can be represented as a sort of 
linear combination of this basis (for instance, in terms of how much it 
contains on each subject. e.g. any information on economics, philosophy, 
nanotechnology, cryonics, etc in it. the basis is how much
you like an average message to have on each subject) the differentiation 
comes in during the basis choosing phase. Relevance is used
to compute differences between messages and the basis,  and the norm
of the coordinate vector can be used to choose by how much to move the
basis.

(*) I am not suggesting that this is a real basis in a linear
algebra sense, only that it is semi-basis like. I use it only
as a metaphor.

  I am still shopping around for various collaborative filtering
schemes. The various vector techniques are economic, but may not pack
the punch needed for good filtering. I'm also looking at fuzzy
logic expert systems and specialized language parsing techniques.

  Another idea I had was to create a futures market based around
message ratings. The software will try to predict what the rating
will be, and various people will bet on whether the actual rating
is higher or lower. There would be a real payment in terms of list
credits (used to defer list subscription fees) Thus, if you want
more filtering, you must put more of your list credit capital
on the line, but if you lose too much you'd have to send in more 
money to continue your subscription. If you win a lot, you'd get
a free ride. You could filter based on two techniques: 1) the predicted
software rating 2) the market value (expected) of the post.
The scenario would look like this:

1) A new Tim May post arrives, software predicted rating 9.2, some
contracts are issued
2) you feel that the real rating is 8.1 and you believe most people
will rate it that way
3) you buy "the final rating will be lower than 9"
4) after the contract gets called in, if you are left holding
"rating will be lower than 9" and the actual rating is below 9,
then you get some payoff for the number of contracts you hold
derived from the money that the losers had to pay
5) contracts can be bought and sold, but the list will not buy them
back

  Since the list is based on agents, and there can be any number of
filtering systems, all of these systems can be ran at the
same time and you choose the one you want. Personally, I kinda  
like the futures market one, cause it would be fun and it would serve
a purpose. (make money + filter based on market consensus)

-Ray
